{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer","id":422999,"title":"Roanoke, Year Without a Summer","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-02-28T09:04:28-08:00","id":422999,"title":"Roanoke, Year Without a Summer","content":"It was so close they're doing both, very excited.<br /> <br /> The Roanoke colony, the &quot;Lost Colony&quot;, that mysteriously disappeared, eh? Work of a Kerboroi perhaps? Cthonian? Worse?<br /> <br /> Beyond that I'm really curious about this one being Geist specific.<br /> <br /> Promethean is the Year Without Summer, I looked that up and that's basically when severe abnormal climate caused a major food shortage across the northern hemisphere. Considering Disquiet is this a time when the Prometheans tried to be more active? I am not very familiar with Promethean so I am not particularly sure about this one outside Disquiet.<br /> <br /> And Hunter the Book of Judges didn't win...<br /> <br /> So Werewolf and Rome...<br /> <br /> Finally, since the Dark Eras Kickstarter is coming to a close looking over all the eras we got, I gotta say holy sheep sh!* that's a lot of eras.<br /> <br /> EDIT*<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2111-doeman","user":"DoeMan","date":". 02-28-2015, 12:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2111-doeman","name":"DoeMan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"68"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-28T09:09:15-08:00","id":423018,"title":"","content":"They actually had doing the first and second place winners on the schedule when they opened the poll.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-28T09:22:19-08:00","id":423030,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DoeMan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer#post422999\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Promethean is the Year Without Summer, I looked that up and that's basically when severe abnormal climate caused a major food shortage across the northern hemisphere. Considering Disquiet is this a time when the Prometheans tried to be more active? I am not very familiar with Promethean so I am not particularly sure about this one outside Disquiet.</div> </div> </div> </div>Disquiet leads to climate and nature changes - called Wasteland. The <b>Year Without Summer</b> sounds as it was result of some abnormal activity of Prometheans. It's also the year Mary Shelley starts the work on the <i>Frankenstein; or, The Modern Prometheus - </i>maybe when she and Percy Bysshe Shelley, visited Lord Byron at the Villa Diodati by Lake Geneva in Switzerland - they encounter Frankenstein's Linage Progenitor, wanting to get a sleep. And in that old, dark summer night he told them his story. And the young writer just changed some facts about?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 02-28-2015, 03:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-28T10:17:36-08:00","id":423085,"title":"","content":"I had the Villa Diodati in mind when I first suggested it - the volcanic weather being a sign of great Disquiet fits as well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Craig Oxbrow<br /> <b>The Trinity Continuum</b> freelancer","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/221-craig-oxbrow","name":"Craig Oxbrow","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar221_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"608"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-28T10:32:59-08:00","id":423094,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer?p=423030#post423030\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Disquiet leas to climate and nature changes - called Wasteland. The <b>Year Without Summer</b> sounds as it was result of some abnormal activity of Prometheans. It's also the year Mary Shelley starts the work on the <i>Frankenstein; or, The Modern Prometheus - </i>maybe when she and Percy Bysshe Shelley, visited Lord Byron at the Villa Diodati by Lake Geneva in Switzerland - they encounter Frankenstein's Linage Progenitor, wanting to get a sleep. And in that old, dark summer night he told them his story. And the young writer just changed some facts about?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> It was a drought caused by a volcano, so if one really needed to blame something supernatural I'd go with a firestorm or a Quashmallam, since I don't remember accidental volcanic eruptions listed in wasteland effects.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-28T18:41:28-08:00","id":423671,"title":"","content":"For inspirational reading on The Year Without a Summer I highly recommend Warren Ellis's short comic <i>Frankenstein's Womb</i>, in which he puts forth the hypothesis that Mary Shelley was the most influential science-fiction writer of the 1800s.<br /> <br /> It's also narrated by The Monster.<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://encrypted-tbn1.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcS0wnwadQ41U6k81s9vY5yXMWDBW09ZsQhATmKfKl3T3qHWlavP\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Cxuog44JenzbN7GaAyRqDO4DXce9VGv6ybaCv9yYsPk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Simulacre: An Alternative Morality Stat for Prometheans</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/9523-the-alchemy-lab-homebrew-athanors\" target=\"_blank\">Homebrew Athanors</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/887-metamurpho","name":"Metamurpho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar887_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"228"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-28T19:04:27-08:00","id":423677,"title":"","content":"Well, I'm pretty sure the Year Without A Summer was picked just because Mary Shelly was in it...<br /> <br /> Still, the Year Without A Summer was a momentous year. It was a time of great upheaval in Europe, what with Napoleon once again returning to Paris and, with the anger of the French People at their restored king palpable, the army actually backed him in a coup d'etat rather than run him out of town. <br /> <br /> I've seen theories that the Year Without A Summer was a direct player in Napoleon's return, as the numerous crop failures from the unusual weather exacerbated the social strain at the time, and people were ready for another shot at that whole revolution thing, rather than just sit there and starve to death. Unfortunately for them, this was a rather short-lived crack at revolution, as it's the one with Waterloo in it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5120-wraith_magus","name":"Wraith_Magus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"96"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-28T23:57:52-08:00","id":423883,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wraith_Magus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer?p=423677#post423677\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, I'm pretty sure the Year Without A Summer was picked just because Mary Shelly was in it...</div> </div> </div> </div>And yet, that's an incredibly good reason to do that year for <b>Promethean</b>. Mary Shelley claimed that <i>Frankenstein</i> was based on a nightmare she'd had on the trip, one where she'd &quot;awoken&quot; and saw a horribly scarred man at her window, glaring in at her . . . .<br /> <br /> What's even <i>more</i> significant is that she'd just been touring the hometown of one Conrad von Dipple. You see, (in the real world) he'd been a crazed alchemist and grave robber, one who'd pursued the dream of immortality via macabre experiments such as boiling bones down . . .<br /> <br /> What if, in the World of Darkness, <i>Frankenstein</i> wasn't fiction? What if a Promethean (possibly created by von Dipple, or von Dipple himself) saw the young writer, and decided to pull an <i>Interview with the Vampire</i>? It would certainly explain how Shelley (an unpublished writer) trumped all of the other authors in the contest. . .<br /> <br /> <br /> <i><u>Edit</u></i>: Alternately, maybe Mary Shelley <i>herself</i> was creating Prometheans. Mur Lafferty, a former White Wolf author who's returning for the <b>Wraith 20th Anniversary</b> release, proposed (in <i>Shambling Guide to NYC</i>) that <i>Frankenstein</i> is actually Mary Shelley's well-concealed research notes","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4624,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","user":"One Vorlon","date":". 03-01-2015, 03:01 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T00:27:40-08:00","id":423891,"title":"","content":"I don't want to diminish Mary's creative genius though by the suggestion she needed help to best the other writers.... oooo... what if we've got the chronology slightly wrong, and there was a second doctor present, other than Polidori - and rather than art imitating life, life imitated art, and Mary INSPIRED the obsession of a Demiurge.<br /> <br /> Of course the opening fiction to Promethean: the Created implies that she was informed of the story (or a version of it) by the Bride - the Sublimatus born of the Monster's (or the Doctor's) failed attempt to create a mate. So presumably the first ever Torch-Born (though, in my own history of the setting, Torch-Born predate the Frankenstein Lineage, thanks to the Belenus &quot;Wickermen&quot; who date back to the Ancient Celts, but the last of whom died in 1666).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":1098,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","user":"glamourweaver","date":". 03-01-2015, 03:45 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T13:42:37-08:00","id":424438,"title":"","content":"Well, call it my own biases, but I honestly prefer not to play too much to the stereotypes. <br /> <br /> I don't find playing a fundamentalist nutbag &quot;if she weighs less than a duck, she's a witch!&quot; witchunter appealing. I don't find people who actively follow the folktales to deliberately be a fairy tale creature as much as possible in Changeling appealing. And just overtly re-enacting some famous movie is not appealing to me. <br /> <br /> Rather, Frankenstein, as it has come down through the years, has had its &quot;Divine Fire&quot; rewritten with every generation. Lightning was the original, because at that time, electricity was the newest buzzword. Scientists were doing experiments where they would shock a dead frog, and see its legs twich, and wonder why. Hence, to the uninformed, &quot;Lightning + !!SCIENCE!! = Resurrection&quot;. <br /> <br /> When it came to the classic movie, it wasn't lightning, it was radiation, because we all knew what electricity did, and you couldn't BS people into believing you could just slap corpse parts together and then hit it with a defibrillator to make a new person. And hey, back then, radiation was MAGIC! It could make ants grow into colossi! If it were redone now, you'd pull out the latest pseudo-science-as-magic buzzwords, instead - nanomachines, genetically engineered stem cells, quantum something-something!<br /> <br /> Beyond that, the core tenant of the story was the evils of science as &quot;playing God&quot;. At that time, even the concept of medicine as a science was controversial. At that time, the simple idea that one could actually heal a disease was seen as a transgression upon God's domain, whereas we actively look upon ideas of refusing blood transfusions because of worries that it might taint the souls of the people involved as insane ramblings today. In the recent past, the new front was in vitro fertilization, but now that's accepted. Now we're talking genetic modification, especially as it relates to curing genetic diseases as Playing God.<br /> <br /> This was, ultimately, why I never liked the story of Frankenstein - it's always been anti-rationalist propaganda. <br /> <br /> That isn't to say that Promethians, themselves, can't be compelling characters. But it's always been as a de facto rebuttal to this story. Being able to escape the same constant plotline by moving beyond it.<br /> <br /> Hence, I actively dislike the &quot;Mary Shelly Promethian&quot;, or &quot;Cleopatra Mummy&quot;, or &quot;Spanish Inquisition Hunter&quot;, and have misgivings about &quot;Grimm Fairy Tales Changeling.&quot; It's throwing the gamelines into a box, and saying they're not allowed to be actual explorations of the themes of their subject matter viewed through different lenses, no, they're stuck just retelling the same story over and over again. (If there were a Mummy set in Asia or Mesoamerica for that matter, it could include rules for a non-Phaoronic mummy, and I might actually be more interested in the game...)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5120-wraith_magus","name":"Wraith_Magus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"96"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T13:55:36-08:00","id":424451,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wraith_Magus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer?p=424438#post424438\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When it came to the classic movie, it wasn't lightning, it was radiation, because we all knew what electricity did, and you couldn't BS people into believing you could just slap corpse parts together and then hit it with a defibrillator to make a new person. And hey, back then, radiation was MAGIC! It could make ants grow into colossi! If it were redone now, you'd pull out the latest pseudo-science-as-magic buzzwords, instead - nanomachines, genetically engineered stem cells, quantum something-something!</div> </div> </div> </div>literally what the fuck are you talking about<br /> <br /> The two &quot;classic&quot; Frankenstein movies came out in 19<i>10</i> and 19<i>31</i> and respectively involved a vat of chemicals and electrical devices; the heyday of Magic Radiation was the 19<i>50s</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":". 03-01-2015, 04:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T14:08:03-08:00","id":424487,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wraith_Magus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer?p=424438#post424438\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Beyond that, the core tenant of the story was the evils of science as &quot;playing God&quot;. At that time, even the concept of medicine as a science was controversial. At that time, the simple idea that one could actually heal a disease was seen as a transgression upon God's domain, whereas we actively look upon ideas of refusing blood transfusions because of worries that it might taint the souls of the people involved as insane ramblings today. In the recent past, the new front was in vitro fertilization, but now that's accepted. Now we're talking genetic modification, especially as it relates to curing genetic diseases as Playing God.<br /> <br /> This was, ultimately, why I never liked the story of Frankenstein - it's always been anti-rationalist propaganda.</div> </div> </div> </div>I completely disagree with this take. The core tenets of Frankenstein are about acceptance of the strange, and taking responsibility for what you do. In many ways it can be seen as a criticism of the European view on God, in that he created man, and then set him free to do whatever he wanted.<br /> <br /> And when Frankenstein was written, people had started to use electricity to stimulate dead muscles, and it wasn't at all seen as impossible that with electricity people could be revived. Just because we know better now, it doesn't make the bleeding edge of that age's technology anti-rationalist.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/585-seidmadr","name":"Seidmadr","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar585_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T14:17:39-08:00","id":424496,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hence, I actively dislike the &quot;Mary Shelly Promethian&quot;, or &quot;Cleopatra Mummy&quot;, or &quot;Spanish Inquisition Hunter&quot;, and have misgivings about &quot;Grimm Fairy Tales Changeling.&quot; It's throwing the gamelines into a box, and saying they're not allowed to be actual explorations of the themes of their subject matter viewed through different lenses, no, they're stuck just retelling the same story over and over again. </div> </div> </div>I don't know about Cleopatra Mummy (have no idea bout that game line), but &quot;Mary Shelly Promethean&quot; as you call it seems interesting for more things besides that footnote about Villa Diodati (wich is cool too!) and ties into the themes of the game in similar ways at the other Dark Era: the Dustbowl (natural disasters that basically turned whole nations into Wastelands and the social unrest that went with that. Bad times for being Human, even more so to try to <i>became</i> one).<br /> <br /> Spanish Inquisition Hunter actually puts a twist in that the Inquisition is going to be going agaisnt the Hunters as the Vigil as a lifestyle rises a lot of red flags that can be confused with hereticism.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T15:16:49-08:00","id":424591,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer?p=424451#post424451\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">literally what the fuck are you talking about<br /> <br /> The two &quot;classic&quot; Frankenstein movies came out in 19<i>10</i> and 19<i>31</i> and respectively involved a vat of chemicals and electrical devices; the heyday of Magic Radiation was the 19<i>50s</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not the heyday or not, while there certainly were chemicals and electrical devices, the quintessence/&quot;secret to life&quot; was &quot;the power of the atom&quot;, not just electricity. They made me watch it (the 31 version) as part of a college class for illustration of this specific topic relating to ethics in science, actually. Radiation had a longer shelf-life (half-life?) in popular culture than you think. It basically started with Marie Curie's work with radiation at the turn of the century, and from there, you had <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoe-fitting_fluoroscope\" target=\"_blank\">people offering the chance to blast yourself with x-rays as a gimmick to sell shoes</a> as far back as the 1920s.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5120-wraith_magus","name":"Wraith_Magus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"96"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T15:57:48-08:00","id":424619,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Seidmadr</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer?p=424487#post424487\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I completely disagree with this take. The core tenets of Frankenstein are about acceptance of the strange, and taking responsibility for what you do. In many ways it can be seen as a criticism of the European view on God, in that he created man, and then set him free to do whatever he wanted.<br /> <br /> And when Frankenstein was written, people had started to use electricity to stimulate dead muscles, and it wasn't at all seen as impossible that with electricity people could be revived. Just because we know better now, it doesn't make the bleeding edge of that age's technology anti-rationalist.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Basically, we're talking about the most intense period in the clash of <a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/RomanticismVersusEnlightenment\" target=\"_blank\">Romanticism versus Enlightenment</a>, here.<br /> <br /> Following the Protestant Reformation, you had the giant witch scares and social divisions that were played out in deeply religious terms. The couple of centuries of religious bloodshed and witch hunting spawned a backlash in the 17th century in the form of the Enlightenment movement, which tried to push people away from religious understanding of the world, towards a more scientific one. The themes they generally embraced was one that all the universe could be observed and manipulated through science and rationality. THIS caused its own backlash in the form of Romanticism in the 18th and 19th centuries, specifically pushing back at the evils of the Industrial Revolution in general, but also the ideals of the Enlightenment and its knowability of the world. Lovecraft wouldn't come around for a century, but Romanticism as a movement spawned the earliest forms of most of our modern horror tropes (including Vampires and Edgar Allen Poe) during the 19th century, where exploration of the unknown was generally seen as dangerous, evil, and the work of madmen, rather than heroic promotions of the frontiers of humanity.<br /> <br /> In fact, if you want to get down to it, the whole &quot;Law Versus Chaos&quot; thing you see recycled in D&amp;D spawns from this movement, through Der Ring des Nibelungen, as Law being the force of Rationalism and Chaos being Romanticism. (With Wagner staunchly on the side of Romanticism, and openly fetishizing Gotterdammerung as a nearly Communist-style overthrow of Industrialism.)<br /> <br /> ... Which is all part of why I really wish they'd assemble this book chronologically, and have just a few paragraphs to tie in the general sweep of history, such that people could get more out of the historical context of all this...<br /> <br /> In any event, Mary Shelly was very much a part of the Romanticist, anti-rationalist wave. The subtitle of the original Frankenstein was &quot;The Modern Prometheus&quot; (gee, wonder where the gameline got its title...) as a specific allegory to the nature of how the monster was created as an attempt to <i>steal</i> the powers of God, as a mad trespass. Frankenstein has been used as practically the defining example of the debates over ethics in science specifically because of its overt themes that have basically been the crux of every argument against research that have been laid down before or since. <br /> <br /> Now, you might look at it with your own eyes, Death of the Author and all, and put other themes into it, and if you want to do that, then more power to you, you can rewrite the morals of the story to fit your own worldview, and play Promethian to a different theme than the author intended. In fact, I generally find that to be the best way to really enjoy the game, which is sort of why I prefer a Dustbowl version of Promethian to a &quot;Let's dig up Mary Shelly's corpse for one more dance&quot; theme. Still, the original themes of the work were very much part of that general Romanticist zeitgeist, which is <i>precisely why</i> her works were so popular and famous in their day. I could write a whole freaking OTHER giant post or two on how popular villains are generally reflections of the underlying social malaise of the era that produced them, but that's neither here nor there. <br /> <br /> In any event, I much prefer these eras have been used to juxtapose a creature made of the sensibilities of one era against a different era's values. That would give players and storytellers the chance to really expand what the different monsters actually mean as a reflection of humanity, and its past. Vampires as the antithesis of Victorian values is already a part of their modern myth, and part of what makes them interesting is how much our own social rejection of Victorian values has turned them into bizarre anti-heroes (or outright heroes if they sparkle in the daytime and are just moody guys looking to settle down with YOU). Vampires during the Middle Ages, when their myth was entirely different, and about the sins of gluttony rather than lust, were entirely different figures.<br /> <br /> Promethian during, say, the 16th/17th century Englightenment wave that was at the science-rather-than-religion Enlightenment wave that gave us the modern trope of Science Versus Religion could have been more interesting. Promethian Galileo, as some sort of fallout of the schism between science, philosophy, and religion, would have been a much more interesting era. For that matter, skipping ahead to Jules Verne or H.G. Welles, and the re-establishment of science as the weapon of the hero would have made promethians either the ultimate paragons of, or refutations to the zeitgeists of their eras.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5120-wraith_magus","name":"Wraith_Magus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"96"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T15:59:35-08:00","id":424621,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wraith_Magus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer?p=424591#post424591\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Radiation had a longer shelf-life (half-life?) in popular culture than you think.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Doctor Waldman, I learnt a great deal from you at the university about the violet ray, the ultraviolet ray, which you said was the highest color in the spectrum. You were wrong. Here, in this machinery, I have gone beyond that. I have discovered <b>the great ray that first brought life into the world.</b> </div> </div> </div>You can certainly read that line as &quot;it's radiation,&quot; but considering that they're talking about the electromagnetic spectrum and these are also lines in the film\u2026<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Herr Frankenstein is a most brilliant young man, yet so erratic. He troubles me. I'm worried about Henry. Why has he left the university? He was doing so well and he seemed so happy with his work. You know, his researches in the field of <b>chemical galvanism and electrobiology</b> were far in advance of our theories here at the university. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If this storm develops as I hope, you will have plenty to be afraid of before the night's over. Go on, fix the electrodes. This storm will be magnificent. <b>All the electrical secrets of heaven. </b>And this time we're ready. </div> </div> </div>\u2026Yeah, no, this particular thing is pretty clearly not Magic Radiation As Distinct From Electricity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T16:57:57-08:00","id":424670,"title":"","content":"I don't think they were called &quot;scientists&quot; back in the early 19th century yet. I think the term still in use then was &quot;natural philosopher.&quot; (or &lt;whatever branch&gt; philosophy. such as chemical philosophy)<br /> Electricity was still a marvel back then. Later in the mid 19th century, Tesla did some incredible experiments with alternating current, and had ideas that were, even now, amazing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T17:07:46-08:00","id":424687,"title":"","content":"To say nothing about the ideas abut elan vital going on about electricity around the time Shelly wrote Frankenstein...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T19:43:31-08:00","id":424764,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wraith_Magus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer?p=424619#post424619\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In any event, Mary Shelly was very much a part of the Romanticist, anti-rationalist wave. The subtitle of the original Frankenstein was &quot;The Modern Prometheus&quot; (gee, wonder where the gameline got its title...) as a specific allegory to the nature of how the monster was created as an attempt to <i>steal</i> the powers of God, as a mad trespass. Frankenstein has been used as practically the defining example of the debates over ethics in science specifically because of its overt themes that have basically been the crux of every argument against research that have been laid down before or since.<br /> <br /> Now, you might look at it with your own eyes, Death of the Author and all, and put other themes into it, and if you want to do that, then more power to you, you can rewrite the morals of the story to fit your own worldview, and play Promethian to a different theme than the author intended. In fact, I generally find that to be the best way to really enjoy the game, which is sort of why I prefer a Dustbowl version of Promethian to a &quot;Let's dig up Mary Shelly's corpse for one more dance&quot; theme. Still, the original themes of the work were very much part of that general Romanticist zeitgeist, which is <i>precisely why</i> her works were so popular and famous in their day. I could write a whole freaking OTHER giant post or two on how popular villains are generally reflections of the underlying social malaise of the era that produced them, but that's neither here nor there.</div> </div> </div> </div>If we're going Death of the Author here, I'd say you're relying more on it than any critic who awkwardly raises the point that Ms. Shelly wasn't that anti-science.<br /> <br /> Part of the main theme of Frankenstein is that the Monster could have been good, or at least he thinks so. He's certainly brilliant; he taught himself a human language and became quite fluent in it simply by watching a human family. Frankenstein himself is responsible for his Creature's actions, in that he was utterly unwilling to deal with the responsibility of the knowledge he brought. The scientist is a modern Prometheus, but his a pretty piss-poor substitute for the Greek fire-giver. Frankenstein <i>hoards </i>his knowledge, squanders it, even on his deathbed he thinks it's devauled if he chooses to share it with Walton (all he says is that electricity is involved, but not how). Hence, the genie goes back in the bottle, because it was never let out in the first place. Victor shouldn't be playing God because of some strange idea of cosmic order-it's that he in particular is really bad at it, and doesn't actually consider the ramifications of doing so. Hence why I like to say <i>Young Frankenstein </i>is the most faithful adaptation of the book, because its own inference (that a Frankenstein who cared for his Creature ended up in a much better place at the end) is entirely logical given the themes and actions of the book's characters.<br /> <br /> And more importantly, the context. Shelly was a suffragette before their time, and had suffered a stillbirth before that point. Frankenstein creates life to prove he doesn't need a woman to do so, and not only does that prove a poor idea for the child, he also evades his responsibilities as a father, thus turning the book into an indictment of deadbeat dads and a metaphor for stillbirth. Also, her professed belief that <i>any </i>excess of obsession invariably ends tragically...in reference to <i>politics.</i><br /> <br /> I'm not saying there isn't &quot;men choosing to steal god's work&quot; there, but it's a lot more nuanced than what you seem to think. And of course, there's also the fact that Promethean alters one of the themes completely; the Monster's desire to become human isn't a hopeless quest at all. That flips one of the morals on its head right there.<br /> <br /> As for Roanoke, I hope they bring up the fact it's the most likely explanation is that the survivng colonists moved over to live with the Chowanoke and interbred so much it was impossible to tell any English ancestry among their children. Could raise some interesting themes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":180,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","user":"Leliel","date":". 03-02-2015, 12:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T20:15:30-08:00","id":424803,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leliel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer?p=424764#post424764\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If we're going Death of the Author here, I'd say you're relying more on it than any critic who awkwardly raises the point that Ms. Shelly wasn't that anti-science.<br /> <br /> Part of the main theme of Frankenstein is that the Monster could have been good, or at least he thinks so. He's certainly brilliant; he taught himself a human language and became quite fluent in it simply by watching a human family. Frankenstein himself is responsible for his Creature's actions, in that he was utterly unwilling to deal with the responsibility of the knowledge he brought. The scientist is a modern Prometheus, but his a pretty piss-poor substitute for the Greek fire-giver. Frankenstein <i>hoards </i>his knowledge, squanders it, even on his deathbed he thinks it's devauled if he chooses to share it with Walton (all he says is that electricity is involved, but not how). Hence, the genie goes back in the bottle, because it was never let out in the first place. Victor shouldn't be playing God because of some strange idea of cosmic order-it's that he in particular is really bad at it, and doesn't actually consider the ramifications of doing so. Hence why I like to say <i>Young Frankenstein </i>is the most faithful adaptation of the book, because its own inference (that a Frankenstein who cared for his Creature ended up in a much better place at the end).<br /> <br /> And more importantly, the context. Shelly was a suffragette before their time, and had suffered a stillbirth before that point. Frankenstein creates life to prove he doesn't need a woman to do so, and not only does that prove a poor idea for the child, he also evades his responsibilities as a father, thus turning the book into an indictment of deadbeat dads and a metaphor for stillbirth. Also, her professed belief that <i>any </i>excess of obsession invariably ends tragically...in reference to <i>politics.</i><br /> <br /> I'm not saying there isn't &quot;men choosing to steal god's work&quot; there, but it's a lot more nuanced than what you seem to think. And of course, there's also the fact that Promethean alters one of the themes completely; the Monster's desire to become human isn't a hopeless quest at all. That flips one of the morals on its head right there.<br /> <br /> As for Roanoke, I hope they bring up the fact it's the most likely explanation is that the survivng colonists moved over to live with the Chowanoke and interbred so much it was impossible to tell any English ancestry among their children. Could raise some interesting themes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh thank goodness. I get quite tired sometimes of the repeated inference that Frankenstein's primary moral is &quot;man should not meddle in God's domain&quot;. It's a possible reading, but the book actually offers a myriad of different, and far more complex, readings on how and why events pan out the way they do. And honestly, most of those reasons seem to revolve around Frankenstein's (and a few other humans') automatic revulsion at the physical appearance of the creature's form rather than anything to do with transgressions against a higher cosmic order.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T20:25:47-08:00","id":424808,"title":"","content":"From my understanding of the situation, which is based on a 4000 level Victorian Lit class, Frankenstein is an allegory for the universal panning of Percy's literary works up to that point. His works were ill received, and he was mooching off of Byron and his largesse to survive. Mary thought they were undervaluing her husband's genius. Percy is Frankenstein, a genius, frantic with creativity and potential, and the Monster is all of his writing. Naturally, we remember Percy Shelly in a much better light, considering Mary spent the last half of hers changing his legacy.<br /> <br /> Mary Wollstonecraft, Mary's mother also raised her daughter on some insanely liberal ideas, for the time. Considering ths is coming from the woman who <i>Vindication</i><i> of the Rights of Women</i>, there is very little chance that Shelly intended <i>Frankenstein </i>as anything but science positive.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2861-nyx","name":"Nyx","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2861_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-02T14:26:17-08:00","id":425668,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>StSword</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer?p=423094#post423094\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> It was a drought caused by a volcano, so if one really needed to blame something supernatural I'd go with a firestorm or a Quashmallam, since I don't remember accidental volcanic eruptions listed in wasteland effects.</div> </div> </div> </div>And honestly I find the year without a summer more interesting not as a <i>result </i>of wasteland, and more as a <i>cloak</i> for it. This is a year or so where everything, landscape-wise, is already completely fucked. Wastelands will not be nearly as noticeable, so prometheans can maybe operate a bit more freely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":996,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","user":"Gabby","date":". 03-02-2015, 05:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","name":"Gabby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-02T17:57:08-08:00","id":425870,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer?p=424621#post424621\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You can certainly read that line as &quot;it's radiation,&quot; but considering that they're talking about the electromagnetic spectrum and these are also lines in the film\u2026<br /> <br /> \u2026Yeah, no, this particular thing is pretty clearly not Magic Radiation As Distinct From Electricity.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not only <i>can</i> you read that &quot;ray beyond ultraviolet&quot; as meaning radiation, it's the only <i>possible</i> way to read it. They just thought you could make gamma rays (or maybe magic &quot;life rays&quot;) with a modified toaster or microwave, that's all. (The difficulty of producing gamma radiation from just having a really fancy heating coil wasn't yet known, certainly to a layman audience.)<br /> <br /> So you're saying that my argument that they were using poorly-understood concepts relating to radiation is wrong because the terms they are using indicates how poorly they understand what radiation even is?<br /> <br /> ... I'm sorry, how does that disprove what I said?<br /> <br /> It's no different than the <a href=\"http://www.cracked.com/article_19160_8-scenes-that-prove-hollywood-doesnt-get-technology.html\" target=\"_blank\">use of scenes where two people in a TV show can &quot;Hack Faster&quot; if they both type on the same keyboard at the same time</a>. It relies upon people not knowing what any of that is, but knowing that it's somehow &quot;sciency&quot;, and therefore <i>scary</i> to work without going into details.<br /> <br /> In any event, if you want to continue that line of argument, take it up with my ethics in engineering professor, because he had a pretty lengthy lecture on the subject...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5120,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5120-wraith_magus","user":"Wraith_Magus","date":". 03-02-2015, 11:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5120-wraith_magus","name":"Wraith_Magus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"96"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-02T18:24:35-08:00","id":425936,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leliel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer?p=424764#post424764\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If we're going Death of the Author here, I'd say you're relying more on it than any critic who awkwardly raises the point that Ms. Shelly wasn't that anti-science.<br /> <br /> Part of the main theme of Frankenstein is that the Monster could have been good, or at least he thinks so. He's certainly brilliant; he taught himself a human language and became quite fluent in it simply by watching a human family. Frankenstein himself is responsible for his Creature's actions, in that he was utterly unwilling to deal with the responsibility of the knowledge he brought. The scientist is a modern Prometheus, but his a pretty piss-poor substitute for the Greek fire-giver. Frankenstein <i>hoards </i>his knowledge, squanders it, even on his deathbed he thinks it's devauled if he chooses to share it with Walton (all he says is that electricity is involved, but not how). Hence, the genie goes back in the bottle, because it was never let out in the first place. Victor shouldn't be playing God because of some strange idea of cosmic order-it's that he in particular is really bad at it, and doesn't actually consider the ramifications of doing so. Hence why I like to say <i>Young Frankenstein </i>is the most faithful adaptation of the book, because its own inference (that a Frankenstein who cared for his Creature ended up in a much better place at the end) is entirely logical given the themes and actions of the book's characters.<br /> <br /> And more importantly, the context. Shelly was a suffragette before their time, and had suffered a stillbirth before that point. Frankenstein creates life to prove he doesn't need a woman to do so, and not only does that prove a poor idea for the child, he also evades his responsibilities as a father, thus turning the book into an indictment of deadbeat dads and a metaphor for stillbirth. Also, her professed belief that <i>any </i>excess of obsession invariably ends tragically...in reference to <i>politics.</i><br /> <br /> I'm not saying there isn't &quot;men choosing to steal god's work&quot; there, but it's a lot more nuanced than what you seem to think. And of course, there's also the fact that Promethean alters one of the themes completely; the Monster's desire to become human isn't a hopeless quest at all. That flips one of the morals on its head right there.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyx</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer?p=424808#post424808\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">From my understanding of the situation, which is based on a 4000 level Victorian Lit class, Frankenstein is an allegory for the universal panning of Percy's literary works up to that point. His works were ill received, and he was mooching off of Byron and his largesse to survive. Mary thought they were undervaluing her husband's genius. Percy is Frankenstein, a genius, frantic with creativity and potential, and the Monster is all of his writing. Naturally, we remember Percy Shelly in a much better light, considering Mary spent the last half of hers changing his legacy.<br /> <br /> Mary Wollstonecraft, Mary's mother also raised her daughter on some insanely liberal ideas, for the time. Considering ths is coming from the woman who <i>Vindication</i><i> of the Rights of Women</i>, there is very little chance that Shelly intended <i>Frankenstein </i>as anything but science positive.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mary Shelly absolutely wrote Frankenstein first and foremost as a horror story relating to &quot;man's transgressions&quot;. You can <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankenstein#Composition\" target=\"_blank\">read right on Wikipedia</a> her own written introduction to the book, which includes the line, &quot;<b>Frightful must it be; for supremely frightful would be the effect of any human endeavour to mock the stupendous mechanism of the Creator of the world.</b>&quot; (Not to mention the movies, where they ham it up on this specific point, about how Frankenstein declares he now &quot;knows what it's like to <b>be</b> a god!&quot;)<br /> <br /> Now, can there have been other motivations, like Nyx is saying about bitterness over her husband's being unappreciated, or a feminist jab? Yes, others, ones that weren't so utterly on-point as the one she specifically listed as her primary inspiration, but that doesn't relegate this to back-bench status. A story can frequently carry with it the social baggage of its day completely without intending to, (like, say, anything with blackface in it...) but that doesn't change the main thrust of a work. But Mary Shelley wasn't being coy about what was supposed to be the horror in this horror story of hers: It's about men playing God, she says it in the introduction in her own words.<br /> <br /> Now, you might not LIKE the facts, you may want to use Death of the Author to insert your own take on it all you want, but that doesn't change that &quot;science is meddling in God's domain&quot; not only was its overt, stated theme, or what everyone recalls about it, but also its primary method of delivering horror. (When people make a movie about a man carrying a baby, it stars Arnold Schwarzenegger, and is <i>very generously</i> called a &quot;comedy&quot;, rather than intending to horrify people with the premise.)<br /> <br /> The horror was that of <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galvanism\" target=\"_blank\">Galvanism</a> actually succeeding, which people feared it would. The idea of raising the dead at a person's whim horrified people, and to the uninformed, it <i>seemed like it might be possible</i>. (And seriously, it's <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/title/tt2918436/\" target=\"_blank\">not like that's gotten old</a>. Again, the only thing that changes is what sort of pseudo-science they blame, because people nowadays know that just sticking a fork in the power socket doesn't bring the dead back to life.)<br /> <br /> Now, is Promethian a different game than Mary Shelley's book?<br /> <br /> Yes, absolutely... because Mary Shelley's book really isn't all that relevant to the story that is being told. The fact that you have to go to such ridiculous lengths to try to apologize for or reinvent the ONE THING everyone knows about this story shows just how severe the cognitive dissonance becomes. That's sort of the point, here. That's <i>why</i> I don't like setting Promethian in Mary Shelly's time, because it chains Promethian to its original creation, and doesn't let it develop on its own. <br /> <br /> Making Aztec Mummy instead of Egypt and supplying rules for how an Aztec mummification can work and be powered would let the game line expand beyond just reliving the same base concept of Egypt. We have a ton of things pulling Vampires out of just what Bram Stoker did. There are different traditions for Promethians, already, detailing the likes of Jewish golems. Promethian's themes have completely outgrown mere fear of lightning. So why does it have to be forced to keep going back to the same Shelley well?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5120,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5120-wraith_magus","user":"Wraith_Magus","date":". 03-02-2015, 11:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5120-wraith_magus","name":"Wraith_Magus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"96"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-03T10:48:51-08:00","id":426702,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/422999-roanoke-year-without-a-summer?p=423883#post423883\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i><u>Edit</u></i>: Alternately, maybe Mary Shelley <i>herself</i> was creating Prometheans. Mur Lafferty, a former White Wolf author who's returning for the <b>Wraith 20th Anniversary</b> release, proposed (in <i>Shambling Guide to NYC</i>) that <i>Frankenstein</i> is actually Mary Shelley's well-concealed research notes</div> </div> </div> </div>It is worth noting that her meticulously kept journals are missing for the period in question. Also, when her journals pick back up again, she makes reference not just to writing but also to translating. Were I to come up with something, I might say that Dipple's research notes were acquired by a member of the Shelley party during their travels, which let to the discussion about the nature of life which supposedly inspired her. She recalled the conversation being between Percy and Byron, but Polidori notes in his journal that it was between Percy and him. Shortly after the ghost story contest, Percy and Byron went on a boating trip for a week. What if Polidori and Mary Shelley attempted the experiment that Dipple never did and succeeded, taking advantage of the incredible thunderstorms the odd weather brought? Like in the book, they were horrified at what they'd done and abandoned the creation. Mary's missing journal entries were the notes of what they did and were taken by Polidori and lost (to make it into the hands of future demiurges). It is also worth noting that Polidori's sister extensively edited his own journals before publication and destroyed the originals.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Eric Christian Berg</b><br /> <i>Onyx Path Freelancer</i><br /> Promethean: The Created Second Edition, Dark Eras Companion","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2019-eberg","name":"eberg","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2019_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"284"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-03T19:36:53-08:00","id":427248,"title":"","content":"Perhaps the original Monster was Conrad von Dippel's (of Castle Frankenstein) creation, but Shelley and Polidori attempted to aide him - resulting in the Bride - the failure of which turned him against them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-03T19:58:00-08:00","id":427265,"title":"","content":"Oooh, that's also compelling.<br /> <br /> Also, if anyone is interested, <i>Stress of Her Regard</i>, by Tim Powers, is an awesome &quot;filling in the blanks with supernatural&quot; story about Byron, the Shelleys, and their circle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Eric Christian Berg</b><br /> <i>Onyx Path Freelancer</i><br /> Promethean: The Created Second Edition, Dark Eras Companion","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2019-eberg","name":"eberg","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2019_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"284"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-03T20:43:40-08:00","id":427332,"title":"","content":"The only problem with having von Dipple as the original Demiurge is that he wouldn't have had a lightning rod or Luigi Galvani's discovery of bioelectric tissue response.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-16T23:19:35-07:00","id":971176,"title":"","content":"Returning to Roanoke, I my advise to fans to watch newest American Horror Story season - it sounds as excatly the Geist story it could be told in ( or about ) this Dark Era. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BwtVKrhjybE\" data-code=\"BwtVKrhjybE\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/BwtVKrhjybE/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> And more light on the background casts IGN with some info compilation on it. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://www.ign.com/articles/2016/09/15/american-horror-story-explaining-the-mystery-of-roanoke\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.ign.com/articles/2016/09/15/american-horror-story-explaining-the-mystery-of-roanoke\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.ign.com/articles/2016/09/15/american-horror-story-explaining-the-mystery-of-roanoke\" target=\"_blank\"> http://www.ign.com/articles/2016/09/15/american-horror-story-explaining-the-mystery-of-roanoke </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 09-17-2016, 02:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","